+# Summary
+
+## Changed
+- `depres/ranges.py`: implemented `satisfies()` and `max_satisfying()` per `docs/RANGES.md`. This covers `||` sets, primitive operators with optional whitespace after the operator, partial versions and X-ranges, hyphen ranges, tilde and caret ranges, and the prerelease rule. Invalid ranges or versions raise `ValueError`. `max_satisfying` returns the original string of the highest match, or `None`.
+- No other files were changed apart from this summary.
+
+## Verified
+- `python -m unittest` (from the project root): 8 tests ran, all OK. They were all erroring before.
+- A throwaway script (since deleted) asserted about 30 extra cases from the doc and passed. It covered caret edge cases (`^0.0.3`, `^0.0`, `^0`), tilde, hyphen with partials (`1.2.3 - 2.3`, `1.2.3 - 2`), the `>1`, `>1.2`, `<=1.2` and `=1.2` table, the prerelease examples, `v` prefixes, build metadata, and `""` and `*`. It also checked that these raise `ValueError`: `>=`, `1.x.3`, `x.2`, `1.2.3 -`, `- 1`, `^`, `1..2` and a bad version string.
+
+## Not verified / unsure
+- **Contract ambiguity:** the doc says `*` and `""` "match any version", but the prerelease rule says a prerelease version needs a comparator naming its `MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH`. I followed the prerelease rule (npm behaviour), so `satisfies("1.0.0-beta", "*")` is `False`. If you want `*` to match prereleases, it's a one-line special case.
+- Not specified in the doc, and my choices follow npm: `>*` and `<*` match nothing, and an empty `||` segment (`"1.x || "`) matches everything.
+- I did not add the extra edge-case checks to `tests/test_ranges.py`.
